            <div xml:id="d390">

                <pb facs="0403" n="391" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg391"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00111A Recruiting/224: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Lockhart</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-06-13T19:00:00-04:00">June 13, 1941—7
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>368. Your 650, June 4. There is no provision of American law prohibiting the
                    enlistment in China of American citizens in the armed forces of Great Britain.
                    However attention is called to provisions of section 401 (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">b</hi>) and (<hi rendition="#italic">c</hi>) Nationality
                    Act of 1940.<anchor n="13" xml:id="d390-f1"/> American citizens enlisted in
                    armed forces of Great Britain who proceed through combat areas violate section 3
                    of the Neutrality Act; also section 5 if they proceed on a belligerent
                    vessel.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
